what is an exit strategy I got a great  question from one of my newsletter  readers Marquez who wrote in to say he's  starting a car rental business and he  wants to take on investors and wants to  figure out what the exit strategy is  gonna be in four to five years so  Marquez there are three ways to exit a  business and it's a great way to think  about what the exits gonna be before you  bring on investors so you can set up  expectations it may not work out exactly  that way but if people come in with  different expectations then you're not  setting yourself up for success and  bringing on a partner really is like  getting married you have to make sure  you both in it for the right reasons  that you love each other because if  you're in it for different reasons if  you're not super clear and communicating  properly it's not gonna work out and you  do not want an investor in your business  who's not fully on board with the  company it's only gonna cause problems  for you and may sink your company  because you're stuck with them forever  some terms of an exit how do we get out  of this how do we sell our business or  how do we get rid of shareholders three  main ways to do it okay the first is  managing bio so management bio is where  you buy back the shares from the  investor so this could be something that  you prearranged  in advance you know in three years I'm  gonna buy back my shares at this amount  or five years or whatever it is so your  your the company is buying out the  investor at a profitable return for them  the second one is acquisition so you are  acquired by a bigger business who pays  you money and buys out everybody and the  third way to exit is by going public  this is the least common way to exit a  business is you go public so you have a  stock on the public market and investors  buy some of your shares and you're able  to cash out so those are the three main  ways I would again strongly urge you to  think about who you bring on board and  if they're bringing more to the table  than just money  I remember with my first business the  software company and and we got it to a  point we're having some success and felt  like we needed either an investor  or some guidance to help us get it to  the next step and we had a guy who  helped us we met with who talked the  talk talked a big talk at a great  downtown office and wanted to be a  partner in the business and something  just felt wrong with it and ended up not  working out with him and we felt regret  you know we felt this might have been  our shot but it just didn't feel right  he just didn't fit the culture of the  business and again if you get stuck with  the wrong partner wrong investor it will  sink your company because they're  they're super hard to get rid of new  investors don't want to come in to buy  out existing investors when I was in the  VC business and we got pitched plans and  people wanted to raise dollars to buy  out their angel investors nobody wants  to do that so you're stuck with this  person and so I remember it was such a  hard decision to say no to this person  to end that relationship because we saw  a potential with them but we didn't want  to give up equity it wasn't us being  greedy it just wasn't the right fit for  our business so trying to get caught up  in the excitement I know you need money  and it was hard for us hey I'm making  300 bucks a month it's hard to say no  but it's got to feel right because this  is an important decision it's too  important to bring on the wrong person  so hopefully that helps believe for  those of you watching you liked the  video please give it a thumbs up and I  want to hear from you do you have  questions about raising capital about  how to exit your business leave it below  and I will do a video response thank you  so much and I'll see you soon  you
 

